<PostLayout slug="design-sprints">
	<p>
		A design sprint is a structured way to get from a big question to a tested idea in about a week. The method comes
		from Google Ventures, where Jake Knapp developed it. It brings product, engineering, design and the business into
		one room and moves them through five steps together.
	</p>

	<h2>The five days</h2>
	<PostFigure
		src={sprintDiagram}
		alt="Five-day design sprint diagram: Monday map, Tuesday sketch, Wednesday decide, Thursday prototype, Friday test"
		caption="The five stages of a design sprint."
	/>
	<ul>
		<li><strong>Map.</strong> Agree the long-term goal, map the problem, and choose where to focus.</li>
		<li><strong>Sketch.</strong> Everyone sketches solutions on their own, so the loudest voice doesn't win.</li>
		<li><strong>Decide.</strong> Review the sketches, vote, and the nominated decider makes the call.</li>
		<li><strong>Prototype.</strong> Build a realistic prototype in a day, just real enough to test.</li>
		<li><strong>Test.</strong> Put it in front of five customers and watch what happens.</li>
	</ul>
	<p>
		The value isn't really the speed. It's the discipline. Every activity is timeboxed, and people work alone and in
		parallel instead of debating as a group. That surfaces what the team actually knows, what it's assuming, and where
		the evidence points. The facilitator keeps the process honest.
	</p>

	<h2>When to use one, and when not to</h2>
	<p>
		A sprint is expensive. It takes a week of several senior people's time. I use one for big, risky, high-profile
		decisions with many stakeholders. For everyday work, a short assumption-mapping session and a quick ideas workshop
		do the same job in a couple of hours.
	</p>

	<h2>Sprint one: insurance quote to buy</h2>
	<p>
		RAA's online insurance purchase had a conversion problem at the payment step. The business assumed the fix was
		adding more payment methods. Before committing delivery time to that, we ran a design sprint to test whether it was
		true.
	</p>
	<p>
		I facilitated it alongside my senior manager, and owned the research synthesis, three personas and the business
		case. Before any ideas, we brought five years of existing research together: usability studies, analytics, journey
		maps and third-party reviews. We built in two sessions with senior leaders, one to agree the problem and one to
		review the prototypes.
	</p>
	<p>
		The synthesis showed that around 38 to 40 percent of quotes were ending up deferred because RAA couldn't verify the
		member's identity. The problem was member identity matching, not payment options. That finding changed the
		roadmap, and authenticated quote to buy is now live for motor insurance.
	</p>
