	<h2>What I took from them</h2>
	<p>
		The biggest lesson from both sprints was the same: the most valuable moment came before any sketching. Bringing the
		existing evidence together changed what the team thought the problem was. A sprint that starts from shared evidence
		makes better decisions than one that starts from opinions.
	</p>
	<p>
		I use the same building blocks in everyday work: How might we questions, affinity mapping, silent sketching and dot
		voting. They give everyone's ideas an equal chance, at a fraction of the cost of a full sprint.
	</p>
